The five fields
Identity
Which system, operator, and responsible person — keyed to the existing mark.
Origin
Manufacturer, software, control station, place and time at origination.
Authorization
The authority relied on. A vetting result may be carried; underlying vetting data is never published.
Decision
Human, autonomous, or human-approved autonomous — the rule followed, and the pause-and-confirm at the split.
Action
What actually happened, time and place, observable result — the End Transmission Event, verifiable by outsiders.
Complete or flagged incomplete: a missing field is shown as missing (completeness: "incomplete", missing fields named in the payload). A partial record presented as complete fails the discipline. Subjects in v0.1: drone · evtol · iot-device · digital-content. Vessel subjects are reserved — the maritime attach point is an open research question.
Demo build notes
Signing key
Fresh ECDSA P-256 keypair generated in-browser per load via Web Crypto; address shown above is the first 40 hex chars of SHA-256(public key). Demo-only, labelled, never a real operator key. The seal follows the spec §5 model — sign the record digest, verify by recovery/verification against the claimed identity, nonce strictly increasing per identity from 1. Production records use the EIP-712 typed struct (IOADA) with chainId + verifyingContract, both still TBD in spec §10.
Canonicalization
RFC 8785-style canonical JSON implemented here (recursive key sort, no insignificant whitespace) for every hash input — closing the awareness app's known JSON.stringify gap noted in spec §3.2.
Open items (§10)
Operating entity: OPEN, Jamie's call, funds-gated — the demo does not depend on it. chainId / verifyingContract: demo values above, not production. Transparency Service: not chosen.
SIMULATION ONLY — simulated events, real proofs. No real flight, no real device, no live system, no on-chain transaction, no Transparency Service registration. This is a Phase 0 demonstration of the WorthChain IOADA Record Specification v0.1 (draft, 22 November 2026) — not a filing, standard, endorsement, or claim of adoption by any agency, operator, or platform. It does not claim Part 108 authority and names no pilot, sponsor, or customer.
WorthChain records and attests the NDAA compliance of the drones it tracks — WorthChain itself is not a drone, so the NDAA procurement rules do not apply to it directly. The chain detects tampering; it does not prevent it. WorthChain is a provenance log, not a cryptocurrency and not a control system.
